{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792489596,"nonce":"+wHFSsDvgY89I8smcvNEOFOr3SM=","message":"The return-inference implementation also exposed a shared automatic-release gap: a constructor with a fresh base return and a self-returning recursive branch was not recorded as returning owned storage, so its caller inserted no release. The ownership proof started from no owned-returning functions, which cannot establish a recursive transfer because each recursive branch requires its own fact first.\n\nAutomatic release now starts storage-returning functions as candidates and removes candidates whose return/escape proof fails, rechecking affected callers until stable. Recorded facts remain fixed. Non-candidates cannot re-enter, so this descending proof terminates. Manual release retains its previous single-allocation policy. The existing phi/escape checks still apply. Regression tests verify one caller release for a recursive factory plus borrowed recursive alias, no callee release of returned objects, and borrowed results propagating through an annotated mutual cycle. Shared drop-pass tests (22), expression/control-flow/aggregate/phi regressions (28), and the full LLVM-enabled TypeScript suite pass. The five new recursion tests include actual baseline and LLVM installations and zero-result/unreachable HIR checks for never functions.","files":null}]}